# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3 have "equal_weight → suspense" and "suspense → triumph"; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2 has "suspense → triumph"; they differ when only "the one has no more weight than the other" is true, which the second allows
hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the one has no more weight than the other" is true and "the mind remains in suspense between them" and "that suspense or balance is the triumph of scepticism" are false. Is S4, "The mind must remain in suspense between them; and it is that very suspense or balance, which is the triumph of scepticism.", true then?
hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2 has "wide_of_common_life"; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2 has "equal_weight → suspense"; they differ when only "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life" and "the one has no more weight than the other" are true, which the first allows; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2 has "triumph"; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2 has "wide_of_common_life → suspense ∧ triumph"; they differ when only "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life" and "that suspense or balance is the triumph of scepticism" are true, which the first allows; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3 has "wide_of_common_life → triumph"
hume-dialogues-1.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life" and "that suspense or balance is the triumph of scepticism" are true and "the mind remains in suspense between them" is false. Is the conclusion, "The mind must remain in suspense between them; and it is that very suspense or balance, which is the triumph of scepticism.", true then?
hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2 takes for granted "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life"; hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3 do not
hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-1.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "our arguments lose this advantage and run wide of common life"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
